IMPORTANT NEGATIVE
A global common-mode artifact remains observationally confounded with true shared structure in the current same-time measurement design.

No amount of counting independent sensor families solves a failure mode that is actually global.

The organism needs another source of identifiability.

Candidate source:

TEMPORAL PERSISTENCE DIFFERENCE.

If true developmental structure changes slowly while a global measurement artifact is instantaneous,
time-separated evidence may distinguish them.

That is the next clean question.

DECISION
AR-H10 is POSITIVE for source-family diversity,
with a HARD NEGATIVE for global common-mode noise.

Supported synthetically:

- same-family shared noise can force catastrophic false hereditary growth;
- provenance-aware cross-family evidence prevents family-specific common-mode structuralization;
- persistence gating alone does not solve correlated artifacts;
- global common-mode noise defeats source-family diversity;
- structural-write confidence therefore needs failure-mode independence, not just repeated observations.

Not demonstrated:

- real Yggdrasil sensor-family provenance;
- real shared-noise structure;
- real global-artifact detector;
- temporal disambiguation;
- nonlinear correlated-error geometry.

